# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
p = 'Condensed/build_short_paper.py'
s = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read()


def between(a, b, new):
    """Replace everything from the end of marker a up to the start of marker b."""
    global s
    i = s.index(a) + len(a); j = s.index(b, i)
    s = s[:i] + new + s[j:]


# ---------------- 1 Introduction
between("H1('1. Introduction')\n", "# ============================================================ 2 LITERATURE REVIEW", '''P("Access to formal finance is linked with household resilience, enterprise formation and poverty reduction {1,2}, and mobile phones and digital payment networks have changed how that access is delivered {3,4}, in India supported by public digital identity and payment infrastructure {5,6}. "
  "For a small business or first-time digital customer, an account helps only if it can be used reliably to receive sales proceeds, pay suppliers and manage cash, and used without undue risk. "
  "Financial inclusion here means use of formal financial services: account ownership measures access, digital payment use measures activity. Reported cybersecurity exposure means a report of an unsolicited call or message asking for money, a sign of a possible scam attempt and not verified fraud. The World Bank's Global Findex is the main cross-country source for the first two measures {7,8,9}.")
P("**Problem and gap.** Mobile banking can bring people into the formal system, but each new user is also a possible target and may lack the safeguards of experienced users; deception exploits ordinary behaviour {10,11}, and trust shapes adoption {12,13}. "
  "The literature is strong on mobile-money benefits and on phishing, but rarely joins them in a recent national survey of India that reports adoption, device safeguards and scam exposure together, and little work turns such patterns into an entrepreneurial question: what service could be built, who would pay, and what would make it viable. "
  "We ask where inclusion and cybersecurity risk move together, where they do not, and what institutions and entrepreneurs could do.")
P("**Research questions.** RQ1: How do account ownership, digital payment use and device protection differ by gender, age and income, and how does India compare with peer economies? "
  "RQ2: Are payment users more likely than non-users to report unsolicited money requests, after adjustment? "
  "RQ3: Among people who report a request, is a device lock associated with fewer reporting that they sent money? "
  "RQ4: Which groups show the largest gap between digital payment use and device protection? "
  "RQ5: What do public app reviews, institutional documents and published interview studies reveal about fraud grievances, security friction and support? "
  "RQ6: What price, cost and customer numbers would let an institution-funded safety service break even?")

''')

# ---------------- 2 Literature review
between("H1('2. Literature review')\n", "# ============================================================ 3 METHODOLOGY", '''P("This review covers inclusion and mobile money, adoption and trust, security behaviour and fraud, and cyber-risk economics (35 academic sources), and ends with the gap this study fills.")
H2('2.1 Inclusion, mobile money and development')
P("Deeper finance disproportionately raises the incomes of the poorest {14}, and account access supports inclusive growth depending on how accounts are used {1}. Measurement advanced with the Global Findex {7} and its rounds {8,9,15}, which separated ownership from usage; index studies tied inclusion to income, literacy, urbanisation and infrastructure {16}, and financial illiteracy is widespread {17}. "
  "The best causal evidence is from Kenya: M-PESA helped households share risk {18} and lifted around 2% of households out of poverty, more in female-headed households {2}. Phones cut search costs {3}; mobile transfers improved diets in Niger {19}; debit cards raised savings in Mexico {20}; diffusion differs by institutional context {21}; and digital inclusion is linked with growth {22}. "
  "In India, biometric payments reduced leakage {5} and demonetisation was used to study digital payments {6}. Gains are not automatic: microcredit raised business investment but not average consumption {23}, and digital credit carries promise and risk {24}. Benefits arise when services are used, so this study measures use, not ownership alone.")
H2('2.2 Adoption, trust and fintech')
P("Adoption depends on perceived usefulness and ease of use {25}, plus social influence and facilitating conditions {26}; for mobile banking, credibility, self-efficacy and cost shape intention {12}. Fintech changes industry structure and user expectations {4} and is a driver of inclusion that needs digital identity and interoperable payments {27}, but digital finance has persistent unresolved issues {13}. Safety and trust are conditions for continued use, not extras.")
H2('2.3 Security behaviour, phishing and fraud')
P("Users reject protections that conflict with their tasks {28}. Well-designed fake websites deceive many participants {10}; awareness of phishing risk is unrelated to perceived vulnerability, so people manage familiar risks but not unfamiliar ones {29}; and fraud victims are diverse, with scams exploiting authority, small sums, pressure and trust {11,30}. "
  "Awareness campaigns often fail to change behaviour {31}, though short online education reduced fraud susceptibility in a U.S. experiment, lasting three months only with a reminder {32}.")
H2('2.4 Cyber risk, incentives and the research gap')
P("Security is under-provided because incentives, externalities and asymmetries separate those who bear losses from those who can prevent them {33}; optimal spending depends on vulnerability and potential loss {34}; and cyber risk is a market failure that regulators address through principles {35,36}. "
  "Three conclusions follow: inclusion benefits depend on use {1,2,18}; adoption depends on trust while security failures exploit ordinary behaviour {10,11,12}; and safety is a shared responsibility that markets under-supply {33,35}. "
  "Missing is a recent national view for India that places adoption, device protection and reported scam exposure side by side, adds what users say in their own words, and links the pattern to a venture and its viability. This study addresses that gap.")

''')

# ---------------- 3 Methodology
between("H1('3. Methodology')\n", "H2('3.5 Data analysis')", '''H2('3.1 Research design')
P("The design is cross-sectional, observational and descriptive-analytical: weighted descriptives and group comparisons (RQ1, RQ4); a weighted logistic model (RQ2) and lock-status comparison (RQ3); a peer comparison; qualitative analysis of documents, app reviews and published interview studies (RQ5); and a deterministic financial model (RQ6). "
  "**Justification:** a population survey is the only source recording adoption and exposure for the same adults; interviews of our own were not feasible in a term project, so published studies serve as secondary evidence; and scenario modelling is the standard test of a venture before commercial data exist.")
H2('3.2 Sampling method and participants')
P("We use the India file of the Global Findex Database 2025 (survey year 2024, IND_2024_FINDEX_v02_M) {37}: 3,000 respondents aged 15 or older, a nationally representative adult sample drawn by the World Bank and Gallup (we recruited no one). All percentages use the survey weight (wgt); n and counts are unweighted. The data describe individuals, not businesses. "
  "**Justification:** it is the standard public inclusion benchmark and the only source linking payments to phone-safety items in India.")
H2('3.3 Data collection methods')
P("Seven public sources were used: the India Findex microdata {37}; Findex 2025 country indicators for India and nine peers {38}; Google Play reviews of five payment apps (PhonePe, Google Pay, Paytm, BHIM, SBI YONO; 5,758 reviews, 2 Oct to 19 Nov 2026) {39}; App Store reviews of the same apps (2,090; 30 Apr to 18 Nov) {47}; reviews of eight bank apps in both stores (6,240; 5 Jun to 19 Nov) {48}; three institutional documents {40,41,42}; and five published interview studies {49,50,51,52,53}. "
  "Reviews were collected with google-play-scraper and Apple's public feed; only app, date, rating and text were kept, names were not stored, emails and phone numbers were masked, and reviews under 15 characters and duplicates were removed. The studies (26 to 3,784 participants) were found by targeted search on 20 November 2026 as India-specific, open-access work on fraud, trust or support in digital payments; they are not a systematic review. We conducted no interviews or surveys of our own. "
  "**Justification:** public data permit reproduction, reviews give unprompted user language, and published studies supply victim, merchant and staff experience that reviews rarely contain.")
H2('3.4 Materials and instruments')
P("Python (pandas, numpy, matplotlib) was used for weighted calculations, the model and figures; scripts, an audit file and the review-coding scheme are in the submission folder. Variables: account, anydigpayment (made or received a digital payment in the past year), con1 (phone), con18 (PIN, password or fingerprint lock; 2,141 valid), con21 (unsolicited money request in 12 months; 2,145 phone owners) and con22 (sent money; 219 recipients).")
''')

# ---------------- 3.5 / 3.6
between("H2('3.5 Data analysis')\n", "# ============================================================ 4 RESULTS", '''P("**Survey.** A weighted share is the sum of weights of positive responses over the sum of weights of valid responses, with each subgroup using its own denominator. For RQ2 the outcome is a reported request among phone owners, with payment use, gender, age, education, income quintile, workforce status and urban residence as predictors, estimated by weighted maximum likelihood with an independent-observation sandwich covariance; the public file has no strata or cluster identifiers, so intervals are approximate, and a Kish design effect and assumed extra effects of 1.5, 2 and 3 serve as sensitivity checks. "
  "For RQ4 each group's payment-use share (all adults) is set beside its lock share (phone owners) and, for the central question, its exposure across 18 groups with Spearman rank correlations; denominators differ, so this is descriptive. RQ3 uses a Fisher exact test on unweighted counts, and peer values are compared without tests.")
P("**Qualitative.** Document passages on trust, shared responsibility and credential protection were grouped into themes. Reviews were coded in two steps: dictionaries flagged five themes (fraud or unauthorised transactions; money deducted or failed payment; support and complaints; account blocking, verification or login barriers; safety and trust language) plus security-friction prompts for bank apps, then every fraud-keyword review was read and placed in one of seven categories by one coder. "
  "Blind AI second passes agreed on 112 of 118 Google Play reviews (94.9%, kappa 0.93), 47 of 57 App Store reviews (82.5%, 0.78) and 57 of 65 bank-app reviews (87.7%, 0.84). A human coder outside the group, blind to the labels, re-coded 40 of the 118 Google Play reviews (38 agreed, 95.0%, kappa 0.93) and found no fraud in 60 reviews without fraud keywords; App Store and bank-app coding has no human check. "
  "Bank and payment apps were compared by chi-square tests with Holm adjustment. Findings of the published studies were mapped to eight themes from their full texts by one coder; counts are of studies, not respondents.")
P("**Financial model.** The model is deterministic and illustrative; no input comes from Findex. Base assumptions are a fee of INR 50,000 per institution per month, variable cost INR 10,000, acquisition cost INR 2 lakh and fixed cost INR 3 lakh a month (growing 20% a year), with fees of INR 40,000 and 60,000 in the conservative and optimistic scenarios. Customers grow linearly from zero with no churn; tax, interest and prelaunch spending are excluded. Amounts are in INR lakh (1 lakh = INR 100,000).")
H2('3.6 Ethical considerations')
P("De-identified public microdata are used under the World Bank's access terms and not redistributed; reviews are public with no usernames stored and anonymous quotations; published studies are summarised, not reproduced. Exposure is reported as reports, not verified fraud, and the model is not a risk score or basis for price discrimination. Commercial figures are illustrative and no partner, investor or willingness to pay is claimed. Future interviews or pilots would need informed consent and would not collect credentials.")

''')

# ---------------- 4.5 and 4.7 prose
between("H2('4.5 Public app reviews')\n", "table('cat',", '''P("The datasets hold 5,758 Google Play and 2,090 App Store reviews of five payment apps and 6,240 reviews of eight bank apps (mean ratings 2.90, 3.31 and 2.49). Fraud-keyword reviews were 2.0%, 2.7% and 1.0% of reviews (mean ratings 1.36, 1.51 and 1.22). Failed payments were 2.3%, 1.5% and 0.4% of reviews, support and complaints 5.3%, 6.4% and 5.5%, account blocking or verification 6.3%, 7.8% and 5.9%, and safety and trust language 5.2%, 6.4% and 3.4%. "
  "After every fraud-keyword review was read, 50.8% of Google Play payment-app reviews concerned rewards or cashback not honoured, against 29.8% on the App Store and 4.6% for bank apps, while 38.5% of bank-app reviews concerned blocked accounts, mis-selling, charges or poor grievance handling (Table 5). "
  "Reviews of unauthorised access or theft numbered 6 (0.1%), 2 (0.1%) and 3 (below 0.1%). Security-friction prompts matched 2.4% of bank-app reviews (mean rating 1.59) and 3.9% of payment-app reviews (2.09); in bank apps 69 of 149 mentioned SIM or device binding and 46 biometric or lock prompts, and 104 Google Play payment-app reviews (1.8%) described device-lock prompts, mostly for BHIM. "
  "Bank and payment apps differed after Holm adjustment (p < 0.001) for fraud keywords, failed payments, safety language and security friction, but not for support (p = 0.87) or account blocking (p = 0.12). A Hindi and Hinglish dictionary extension flagged 41 more reviews and would raise unauthorised or unexplained-debit reports on Google Play from 6 to 12 (0.2%).")
''')
between("H2('4.7 Illustrative financial model outputs')\n", "table('fin',", '''P("Contribution per institution is INR 40,000 a month (80%) and simple acquisition payback is 5 months. Steady-state break-even is 8 paying institutions, or 13 when one new institution is acquired each month. The base case records a deficit of INR 32.80 lakh in Year 1, zero in Year 2 and a surplus of INR 52.16 lakh in Year 3; the conservative case is in deficit throughout and the optimistic case reaches INR 48.80 and 173.16 lakh in Years 2 and 3 (Table 6). "
  "Cutting the fee to INR 40,000 raises break-even to 10 institutions; raising variable cost to INR 15,000 raises it to 9; an acquisition cost of INR 3 lakh extends payback to 7.5 months and growing break-even to 15; across fees of INR 40,000 to 60,000 and variable costs of INR 8,000 to 15,000 the requirement is 6 to 12. "
  "Year 3 closing counts of 18, 40 and 65 institutions equal 2.4%, 5.4% and 8.8% of the 741 banks live on UPI in July 2026 {46}.")
''')

# ---------------- Discussion
between("H1('5. Discussion')\n", "H2('5.4 Future scope and limitations')", '''P("**Inclusion versus cybersecurity risk.** The central question has a mixed answer. Payment users reported more unsolicited requests than non-users and the association survived adjustment, so inclusion in digital payments goes with greater reachability by scammers, plausibly because digital users are reachable through more channels and are better targets {30}. "
  "Across groups, however, the link was weak to moderate (rank correlation 0.38): exposure was highest among the most included (tertiary-educated) but also in the lowest income quintile, and lowest among older adults and women, perhaps from fewer contacts or less reporting. Protection rose with inclusion (0.63) except among older adults, whose payment use far exceeds lock use. "
  "Risk therefore concentrates where inclusion outruns safeguards and where people have little margin for loss. A lock limits access to a handset, not deception, which works through trust and routine {10,11,28}; the RQ3 comparison rests on 45 senders and is not significant.")
P("**Reviews, studies and documents.** Review complaints concern rewards, refunds, support, blocks and security prompts far more than theft, on both platforms. This does not measure scam prevalence: victims may not write reviews, and the interview studies describe the social engineering, trust and slow redress that reviews rarely capture. Support and redress failures appear in both sources, consistent with the trust and dispute-resolution themes in the documents {40,41,42}. "
  "In bank apps the tension shows up as friction and weak redress more than fraud: SIM binding, biometric and root-detection prompts were a recurring source of one-star reviews. Against peers and published aggregates {45}, India has near-top account ownership but below-peer phone ownership and digital payment use and the lowest reported request share among economies with a value; recall, reporting and phone-based contact may explain this, and the comparison sets a sample estimate against published values without sampling error.")
H2('5.1 Academic implications')
P("The study joins the economics of inclusion {1,2,18} with the behavioural study of scams {10,11,31}, pairing national statistics with safety indicators and user-generated text to show where access and safe use diverge. It clarifies that the make-or-receive payment indicator differs from the dashboard's made-payment indicator, shows how to label intervals when design variables are missing, and suggests technology-acceptance models {12,25,26} should include perceived safety and device safeguards for continued use.")
H2('5.2 Policy implications')
P("Each implication is a hypothesis to test. Inclusion targets should track active and safe use, not only ownership, in line with the World Bank's link between trust, consumer protection and capability {40,41}. Awareness efforts such as the RBI's {42} should be judged on behaviour change and designed for older, lower-income and women users, since generic campaigns often fail and education persisted in one experiment only with reminders {31,32}. "
  "Providers should own warnings, refunds and complaint handling, regulators clear reporting channels, and users credential hygiene {35,36}; security prompts should be tested so they do not lock out legitimate users. The findings do not support differential pricing or profiling of groups.")
H2('5.3 Managerial implications')
P("The results suggest a narrow pilot of a multilingual payment-safety companion distributed through banks, payment providers or merchant networks, paid for by the institution and free to users: short audio and visual explanations, a pre-payment checklist and verified routes to support and official reporting. It would not hold money, authorise payments, guarantee recovery or ask for an OTP or PIN. "
  "In canvas terms, the customer is the institution, the value is understandable guidance and verified routes to support, revenue is a recurring subscription, and costs are a small fixed product and quality team, variable delivery and customer acquisition. Existing alternatives (RBI and bank campaigns {42}, in-app warnings, the national helpline, in-house builds) already cover parts of the problem, so the service must add measurable value through local-language journeys with comprehension checks, practice outside the transaction flow and faster routes to verified support. "
  "The proposed plan is: months 1–2 discovery with one sponsor and consent-based usability sessions; months 3–5 a controlled pilot with prespecified outcomes; from month 6 scaling only after a paid commitment. No pilot has been run.")
P("**Financial interpretation.** Eight institutions cover fixed costs only in steady state, and about 13 are needed while growing because acquisition is expensed. A 20% lower fee adds two institutions, and the base case has no cushion in Year 2, so churn or slow sales would produce a deficit; the conservative case does not break even in three years. Revenue is not cash, since procurement delays can create a gap. "
  "An illustrative INR 75 lakh seed (staged equity or grant) would cover the Year 1 deficit (32.80), prelaunch build (10.00) and pilot evaluation (5.00), a working-capital reserve (12.00) and contingency (15.20), but in the conservative case deficits plus prelaunch costs reach INR 81.80 lakh by Year 2 and exceed it. "
  "Main risks are weak willingness to pay, support cost above the margin, incorrect advice or compromised links, exclusion or payment friction, and dependence on one partner; each needs evidence from a paid pilot. These conclusions follow security-investment economics {33,34} and hold only if prices and costs are validated with real buyers.")
''')

# ---------------- 5.4 and conclusion
between("H2('5.4 Future scope and limitations')\n", "# ============================================================ REFERENCES", '''P("**Limitations.** The survey is cross-sectional and self-reported; requests are not verified fraud and sending money is not a loss measure. Cells such as the 45 senders are small, intervals are approximate because design variables are missing (the tertiary-education result does not survive assumed clustering), and the data describe individuals, not enterprises. "
  "Reviews are self-selected, cover different windows, and were keyword-coded by one coder with AI second passes (a human check on 40 Google Play reviews only); a Hindi and Hinglish extension changed no conclusion. We conducted no interviews or surveys of our own, and the five published studies were selected by us and are small. Peer values are aggregates without sampling errors, and all financial inputs are unvalidated assumptions without churn, tax or financing flows.")
P("**Future scope.** Priorities are consent-based interviews with merchants, first-time users and budget owners to test willingness to pay; design-based estimation if strata and clusters become available; peer-country microdata and pooled Findex rounds; an evaluated pilot with a power calculation before treating verified loss as an outcome {32}; and a venture model with churn, taxes and cash flow.")

# ============================================================ 6 CONCLUSION
H1('6. Conclusion')
P("This paper asked whether the spread of mobile banking in India comes with higher cybersecurity risk, and for whom. Account ownership is widespread (89.0%), but digital payment use (48.5%) and device protection (57.7%) are lower and unevenly distributed by gender, age and income. Payment users reported more unsolicited requests than non-users (13.1% against 4.3%; odds ratio 3.45), yet across groups exposure rose only weakly with inclusion, was high among the poorest, and protection lagged inclusion at older ages: risk concentrates where inclusion outruns safeguards. "
  "App reviews on both platforms and five published studies show that grievances concern rewards, refunds, account blocks, support and security prompts far more than reported theft, while social engineering and slow redress dominate the interview evidence. "
  "For entrepreneurs and institutions the evidence supports a staged pilot of an accessible, institution-funded safety service; the illustrative model breaks even at eight paying institutions but is highly sensitive to price, support and acquisition costs and is not evidence of demand. Pilot evidence on willingness to pay, retention and effectiveness is the next step.")

''')
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s)
